# Combat-PoD Manual Move Authoring Guide

This guide is the workflow for adding a combat move. 

For the system internals (variable namespace, execution phases, result
codes, tier model, capability flags, engine constraints), see
`COMBAT_REFERENCE.info`. This guide focuses on the
authoring workflow; the reference doc is the source of truth for what
each piece does at runtime.

---

## What a move is

A combat move is a `POD_combat_move` story_cycle. It lives on Charon
(`character:POD_wraith_01`), holds a bag of variables that describe what
the move does, and the execution engine (`pod_combat_move_effects.txt`)
reads those variables at runtime when a combatant uses the move. You
never write engine code to add a move. You declare the move's data and
the engine does the rest.

Authoring a move means producing four artifacts (five for a gated move):

| # | Artifact | What it is | Where it lives |
|---|----------|------------|----------------|
| 1 | A `POD_init_combat_move` call (plus setters) | Builds the move story_cycle and stamps its mechanics | `devpod/common/scripted_effects/POD_combat/pod_combat_move_list_effects.txt` (or a hand-maintained file — see **Quick start**) |
| 2 | A hidden decision | Shows the move's *displayed* unlock requirement in the loadout editor - NOT the real gate (see **Unlock gating**) | `devpod/common/decisions/POD_Combat/pod_combat_move_decisions.txt` |
| 3 | Three localization keys | Display name, dynamic stat-card desc, flavor tooltip | `devpod/localization/english/gui/POD_combat_l_english.yml` (or any hand-maintained `.yml`) |
| 4 | A picture (optional) | The card illustration in the loadout editor and combat bar | One of the `@texture_*` constants at the top of the decisions file |
| 5 | An unlock-dispatcher branch (gated moves only) | The **real** equip / use gate - the trait / perk check, keyed on the move's `SKILL_KEY` under its `CONFIG` | `devpod/common/scripted_triggers/POD_combat/pod_combat_move_unlock_triggers.txt` |

Artifacts 1-3 are mandatory: skip any one and the move fails to load or
shows broken text. Artifact 4 is cosmetic (a decision with no `picture`
renders a generic placeholder). Artifact 5 is what actually gates the
move - omit it and the move is **always unlocked** no matter what the
decision displays, so a gated move needs it, an intentionally open one
does not (see **Unlock gating**).

Artifacts 1-4 are wired together by name: the constructor's `DECISION`
parameter is a decision id, that same id prefixes all three loc keys, and
the engine indexes every move on Charon by its decision id. The dispatcher
branch (5) is keyed separately, on the move's `SKILL_KEY` and `CONFIG`.
Keep the names in lockstep and everything finds everything else.

---

## Quick start

A minimal end-to-end damage move: "Power Punch", an ATK move any
character can equip, dealing 3 HP damage for 4 AP.

### 1. Hidden decision

Append to
`devpod/common/decisions/POD_Combat/pod_combat_move_decisions.txt`:

```
POD_combat_move_power_punch = {
    picture = { reference = @texture_atk }
    is_valid_showing_failures_only = {
        always = yes
    }
    decision_group_type = hidden
    ai_check_interval = 0
    confirm_text = blank_line
}
```

### 2. Localization

Append to a `.yml` under `devpod/localization/english/` (the file must
start with a UTF-8 BOM and the `l_english:` header):

```yaml
 POD_combat_move_power_punch:0 "Power Punch"
 POD_combat_move_power_punch_desc:0 "[PODCombatMoveDesc('POD_combat_move_power_punch')]"
 POD_combat_move_power_punch_tooltip:0 "#weak;italic A brutal haymaker.#!"
```

All three keys are required: the bare display name, the `_desc` that
calls the macro rendering the live stat card, and the `_tooltip` flavor
sentence shown on the move's tooltip card (styled `#weak;italic ...#!`;
use `""` for no flavor line). See **Localization** for what each does.

### 3. Registration

The move story_cycle is built by a `POD_init_combat_move` block plus a
`scope:new_combat_move = { ... }` setter block:

```
POD_init_combat_move = {
    DECISION   = POD_combat_move_power_punch
    SKILL_KEY  = power_punch
    CATEGORY   = ATK
    CONFIG     = base_physical
    AP_COST    = @ap_cost_major
    AI_WEIGHT  = 12
    TARGETING  = single_enemy
}
scope:new_combat_move = {
    POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = checks_guard }
    POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = checks_spirit_gate }
    POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = checks_stance }
    POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = requires_hit_roll }
    POD_combat_move_set_hp_damage = { VALUE = @damage_medium }
}
```

This block has to run once at `on_game_start`. Two homes work - pick on
style:

- **Straight into the move list (simplest).** Paste the block into the
  `POD_initialize_combat_moves` body in
  `pod_combat_move_list_effects.txt`, alongside the existing moves.
- **A separate file (tidier for a few hand-adds).** Put your
  `POD_init_combat_move` blocks in one wrapper effect (e.g.
  `POD_initialize_manual_combat_moves`) in a new file under
  `devpod/common/scripted_effects/POD_combat/`, then call that wrapper
  from `POD_on_game_start` in
  `devpod/common/on_action/POD_vanilla_game_start.txt`, next to the
  existing `POD_initialize_combat_moves = yes` line. This keeps your few
  custom moves easy to find among the hundreds already in the move list.

On the next game start the move is created, added to the global
`POD_combat_moves` list, and appears in the loadout editor. Because this
move is hand-authored, the unlock dispatcher leaves it **ungated** -
anyone can equip and use it, which is what its `always = yes` decision
advertises. (To restrict a hand-authored move, see **Unlock gating**.)
See **Equipping a move** for getting it onto a bar.

If you add the move to a save that already exists rather than a fresh
game, bump `POD_current_combat_version` in
`pod_combat_values.txt`. The configure-skills decision rebuilds
the move catalogue whenever the save's stored version differs from that
constant, and that rebuild is what makes a newly added move appear.

