## The constructor

Every move begins with one `POD_init_combat_move` call. The seven
parameters are all required and are passed as named keys:

| Parameter | Values | Purpose |
|-----------|--------|---------|
| `DECISION` | `POD_combat_move_<key>` | The hidden decision id that holds this move's unlock gate. Must match the decision you author and the loc-key prefix. |
| `SKILL_KEY` | lowercase identifier | Internal flag id, stored as `flag:<key>`. Use the decision suffix without the `POD_combat_move_` prefix. |
| `CATEGORY` | `ATK` / `DEF` / `CTL` / `PWR` | Loadout column. Capped at 4 equipped per category. Drives card coloring and grouping. |
| `CONFIG` | lowercase identifier | Section header in the loadout editor (e.g. `base_physical`, `celerity`, `serpentis`). Free-form flavor grouping. |
| `AP_COST` | integer (typically 1–6) | Action-point cost. Affordability is checked at click time. |
| `AI_WEIGHT` | integer | Base AI selection weight. See **Picking values**. |
| `TARGETING` | one of the targeting flags below | How the move chooses its target. |

The constructor writes `decision`, `skill_key`, `combat_category`,
`config_section`, `ap_cost`, `ai_weight`, and `targeting` onto the new
story, adds it to the global `POD_combat_moves` list, and stores it on
Charon keyed by the decision id. After the call, the separate
`scope:new_combat_move = { ... }` block is where every optional setter
goes.

### Targeting flags

| Flag | Behavior |
|------|----------|
| `single_enemy` | Hits the actor's currently selected enemy. Most attacks. |
| `single_ally` | Hits one ally on the actor's team. Most teammate heals/buffs. |
| `single_pc` | Always targets the player character (team position 0). For NPC moves that specifically protect or attack the PC. |
| `self` | Targets the actor. Self-buffs, stances, AP cantrips, summons. |
| `team_enemy` | AoE. Hits every slot on the enemy team. |
| `team_ally` | AoE. Hits every slot on the actor's own team. |

`team_enemy` / `team_ally` hit **every slot on that team**, whatever its
size. Team size is unbounded (there is no fixed "slots 1–4 / 5–9"
range), so an AoE damage value is applied per-target, not summed across
the team. Size scales the move's total output, so price AoE accordingly.

---

## Setter reference

All setters run inside `scope:new_combat_move = { ... }` after the
constructor. Order is irrelevant with one exception —
`POD_combat_move_set_damage_class` must come after
`POD_combat_move_set_hp_damage`, whose superficial default stamp would
clobber an earlier class write. Grouping by kind (costs, damage,
buffs, flags) keeps the entry readable. Each setter just writes one or
more variables on the story. The rule throughout is **presence =
applies, absence = skip**: a payload fires only if its variable is set.

Numbers can be raw integers or one of the `@`-constants from the top of
`pod_combat_move_list_effects.txt` (e.g. `@damage_minor` = 2). Both are
parse-time substitutions and behave identically — see **Constants
reference**.

### Resource costs

| Setter | Param | Effect |
|--------|-------|--------|
| `POD_combat_move_set_energy_cost = { VALUE = N }` | int | Drains the actor's splat-specific energy pool (Blood / Gnosis / Pathos / Angst / Resolve / Glamour / Dread / Willpower), resolved at runtime from the actor's splat. The decision's trait gate should guarantee a pool exists. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_stress_cost = { VALUE = N }` | int | Adds Mental Strain. PC-only (the AI does not pay it). |
| `POD_combat_move_set_gold_cost = { VALUE = N }` | int | Subtracts gold. PC-only. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_prestige_cost = { VALUE = N }` | int | Subtracts prestige. PC-only. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_piety_cost = { VALUE = N }` | int | Subtracts piety. PC-only. |

### Damage

| Setter | Param | Effect |
|--------|-------|--------|
| `POD_combat_move_set_hp_damage = { VALUE = N }` | int | HP damage to the target. Also stamps the default damage class, `damage_type = superficial`. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_damage_class = { VALUE = X }` | `aggravated`/`fire`/`cold`/`lightning`/`sun`/`silver`/`gold`/`holy`/`balefire` | The move's damage classification (single axis, stored in `damage_type`). `aggravated` forces the wound aggravated for every target and bypasses non-bane soak. An element rides the matching PoD `death_reason` into PoD core, which decides aggravated-ness per target — immunities, downgrades, and splat-weakness multipliers all apply (silver vs werewolves, sun vs vampires, holy vs the unholy). Omit for plain superficial damage. **Call it after `set_hp_damage`** — the hp_damage setter's default stamp clobbers an earlier class write. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_morale_damage = { VALUE = N }` | int | Morale damage. Runs its own auto-hit pipeline, independent of the capability tags and the damage class. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_self_damage = { VALUE = N }` | int | HP damage to the **actor** when the move fires. Superficial, soakable, ignores stances. For reckless attacks. |

#### Pipeline shape via capability tags

There is no runtime "attack type" variable. The shape of the damage
pipeline is composed from four presence flags, each set with
`POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = ... }`:

| Tag | Adds to the pipeline |
|-----|----------------------|
| `checks_spirit_gate` | The incorporeal/spirit-gate check (can't be hit through the wrong plane). |
| `requires_hit_roll` | A hit roll (the attack can miss). |
| `checks_guard` | The target's Guard stance can block it. |
| `checks_stance` | The target's Dodge / Stealth stances can evade it. |

Common shapes:

| Pipeline | Tags | Behavior |
|----------|------|----------|
| **Physical** (full) | all four | Spirit gate + dodge/stealth + hit roll + guard + soak. Can miss, be dodged, guarded, soaked. |
| **Supernatural** (auto-hit) | `checks_stance` only | Auto-hits past hit chance, ignores guard, hits incorporeal; dodge/stealth and soak still apply. |
| **Mental** (morale) | `checks_stance` only, with `morale_damage` instead of `hp_damage` | Same flag profile as supernatural; differentiated by routing through the morale pipeline. |

To make a guard-bypassing physical strike, set the physical tags but
**omit `checks_guard`**. To make an always-hits physical strike, omit
`requires_hit_roll`. There is no `bypass_guard` flag — guard bypass is
the *absence* of `checks_guard`, which the contact resolver reads
directly.

For a pure single-target control with no HP or morale damage, add
`requires_control_contact` together with the intended capability tags.
The engine then runs the same resolver and applies `remove_guard`, tier
payloads, `mind_control`, `ap_drain`, and `initiative_debuff` only on a
successful delivery. Omit this flag for guaranteed utility. The CSV
generator stamps it automatically when such a row has an explicit
`attack_type`; hand-authored moves must set it themselves.

### Healing

| Setter | Params | Effect |
|--------|--------|--------|
| `POD_combat_move_set_hp_heal = { VALUE = N TYPE = X }` | int + `superficial`/`aggravated` | Heals the target. `superficial` refills below the aggravated ceiling only; `aggravated` reduces aggravated wound boxes first, then spills into superficial. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_morale_heal = { VALUE = N }` | int | Restores morale. |

### Conditions: two axes

A move can carry conditions on **two independent axes**. They share one evaluator
(`pod_combat_move_condition_met_trigger`) and the same outcome vocabulary, but answer
different questions - pick by **what you're gating**:

- **Move conditions** (`POD_combat_move_set_condition` / `_set_bonus` / `_set_cond_param`,
  below) gate or buff the **whole move** on **pre-attack state** - round, HP, morale,
  stance, traits, levels, opinion, secrets. **One per move.**
- **Payload riders** (`POD_combat_move_set_heal_condition` / `_morale_damage_condition` /
  `_self_damage_condition`, below) gate **one secondary payload each** on the **attack
  outcome** - `always` / `on_hp_damage` / `on_contact` / `on_miss`. **Independent per
  payload**, so a move can carry a different rider on each of its effects.

Common trap: a "bite that heals on a hit" is the move's **primary** `hp_heal` gated by
`POD_combat_move_set_heal_condition = { VALUE = on_hp_damage }`, **not** a
`POD_combat_move_set_bonus = { EFFECT = heal_target }` (a bonus is an *extra* effect
granted by a state condition, not the move's main payload).

### Rider conditions

A heal, morale-damage, or self-damage payload fires unconditionally by
default. To gate it on the outcome of the move's primary HP-damage step,
set the matching condition flag. Absent = `always`.

| Setter | Values | Gates |
|--------|--------|-------|
| `POD_combat_move_set_heal_condition = { VALUE = X }` | `always`/`on_hp_damage`/`on_contact`/`on_miss` | When `hp_heal` fires. Drain attacks use `on_hp_damage`. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_morale_damage_condition = { VALUE = X }` | same | When `morale_damage` fires. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_self_damage_condition = { VALUE = X }` | same | When `self_damage` fires. |

| Value | Fires when the outcome is |
|-------|---------------------------|
| `always` | any (default) |
| `on_hp_damage` | HP damage landed |
| `on_contact` | any physical contact (landed, guard-blocked, or soaked) |
| `on_miss` | miss / dodge / stealth-evade |

### Conditions (gate)

A move can carry one **state condition** that gates whether it is usable:

| Setter | Effect |
|---|---|
| `POD_combat_move_set_condition = { MODE TARGET PREDICATE THRESHOLD }` | Defines the condition. `MODE = gate` = the move is **usable only while it holds**; `MODE = bonus` = grant a bonus effect when it holds. Re-checked live for PC and AI. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_bonus = { EFFECT VALUE }` | (bonus mode) the effect granted when the condition holds. `EFFECT` = `self_heal` / `heal_target` / `morale_damage` / `damage` / `change_type` / `hit` / `ap_refund` / `ap_drain` / `stance_guard` / `stance_dodge` / `stance_stealth` / `energy_gain` / `energy_drain` / `dot`; `VALUE` = its magnitude (`change_type` / `stance_*` ignore it). |
| `POD_combat_move_set_cond_param = { PARAM }` | (identifier predicates) the literal trait key / secret_type for `has_trait` / `target_known_secret`; the matching trait / secret branch is hand-added to the sub-dispatch in `pod_combat_condition_dispatch_triggers.txt` (see its header). |

- `MODE` = `gate` or `bonus`.
- `TARGET` = `user` | `target` | `global` | `relational` - whose state the predicate reads.
- `PREDICATE`: combat-state (`first_round`, `round_min`, `undamaged`, `hp_missing`, `full_morale`, `morale_missing`, `has_guard`, `has_dodge`, `has_stealth`), character (`prestige_level_min`, `piety_level_min`, `dread_min`, `has_weapon_artifact`, `has_armor_artifact`), relational (`relation_positive`, `relation_negative`), team (`outnumbered`, `ally_knocked_out`), identifier (`has_trait`, `target_known_secret`).
- `THRESHOLD` = the `X` for the `*_min` / `*_missing` predicates + the opinion magnitude (`0` for the boolean ones).
- `PARAM` (identifier predicates, via the cond_param setter) = the literal trait key (`brave`) / secret_type (`secret_diablerist`).

The stat card shows `Only usable if …` (gate) or `Bonus if …:` + the effect (bonus).
Evaluated by `pod_combat_move_condition_met_trigger`; the gate is enforced in
`POD_can_execute_combat_move_trigger` (PC) and `pod_combat_move_is_usable_now_trigger` (AI), the
bonus is evaluated once at execution §2b. All predicate families ship now; the full grammar is in `docs/CONDITION_SYSTEM_DESIGN.md`.

### Tier buffs and debuffs

Eight setters: stat (soak / hit) × direction (buff / debuff) × duration
(round / combat). Each takes `TIER = 1`, `2`, or `3` (small / medium /
large). Buffs apply to the actor except on `single_ally`, where they
apply to the selected ally; debuffs apply to the target.

| Setter | Sets |
|--------|------|
| `POD_combat_move_set_soak_buff_round = { TIER = N }` | `soak_buff_round_tier` |
| `POD_combat_move_set_soak_buff_combat = { TIER = N }` | `soak_buff_combat_tier` |
| `POD_combat_move_set_soak_debuff_round = { TIER = N }` | `soak_debuff_round_tier` |
| `POD_combat_move_set_soak_debuff_combat = { TIER = N }` | `soak_debuff_combat_tier` |
| `POD_combat_move_set_hit_buff_round = { TIER = N }` | `hit_buff_round_tier` |
| `POD_combat_move_set_hit_buff_combat = { TIER = N }` | `hit_buff_combat_tier` |
| `POD_combat_move_set_hit_debuff_round = { TIER = N }` | `hit_debuff_round_tier` |
| `POD_combat_move_set_hit_debuff_combat = { TIER = N }` | `hit_debuff_combat_tier` |

`round` lasts until the holder's own turn boundary (a buff clears at their next turn start, a debuff at their turn end); `combat` lasts the whole fight. A buff/debuff on an attacking move lands *before* that move's own attack, so hit-buff + attack and soak-debuff + attack combos benefit the same hit.
Magnitudes live in `pod_combat_tier_effects.txt`. A higher tier
overwrites a lower one; reapplying the same or lower is blocked by the
redundancy gate.

### Parametric non-tier modifiers

| Setter | Param | Effect |
|--------|-------|--------|
| `POD_combat_move_set_ap_gain = { VALUE = N }` | int | Grants the actor +N AP this turn. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_ap_drain = { VALUE = N }` | int | Drains N AP from the target's next turn. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_initiative_debuff = { VALUE = N }` | int | Subtracts N from the target's next-round initiative. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_retaliate_damage = { VALUE = N }` | int | Sets the counter-attack damage. Needs a `retaliate_if_hit` / `retaliate_if_missed` flag to fire — without one, nothing reads it. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_retaliate_agg = yes` | — | Marks the counter-attack as aggravated. For an elemental counter instead, give the move an elemental damage class via `set_damage_class` (the counter reuses it). Kind precedence: morale > `retaliate_agg` > elemental class > plain superficial. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_retaliate_morale = yes` | — | The counter erodes morale instead of HP (amount from `retaliate_damage`). A morale counter has no damage kind, so it wins over the aggravated / elemental marks if both are set. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_dot = { AMOUNT = N ROUNDS = N }` | two ints | Seeds a burn that ticks `AMOUNT` damage at each round end for `ROUNDS` rounds. The tick's kind comes from the move's damage class (`aggravated` ticks aggravated, an element rides its death_reason, superficial ticks plain duel damage); the `dot_morale` flag overrides to morale. Orthogonal to direct damage — a move can hit and burn. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_steal_gold = { VALUE = N }` | int | **PC-only.** At execution §4, steal up to N gold from the target (clamped to its gold) into the actor's purse. |
| `POD_combat_move_set_gain_dread = { VALUE = N }` | int | **PC-only.** At execution §4, the actor gains N dread (0-100 cap). |
| `POD_combat_move_set_once_per = { DURATION = round \| combat }` | flag | Caps the move to once per actor per round / per combat (per-slot used-move list, enforced in both usability gates). |

### Presence flags

A presence flag means "this attribute is set." All are written with the
generic setter:

```
POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = <flag> }
```

| Flag | Effect |
|------|--------|
| `set_guard` | Activates Guard on the actor, or on the selected beneficiary for `single_ally` (next physical attack against them is blocked). |
| `dodge` | Activates Dodge on the actor, or on the selected beneficiary for `single_ally` (next attack auto-misses). |
| `stealth` | Activates Stealth on the actor, or on the selected beneficiary for `single_ally` (next incoming attack auto-misses; holder's next attack gets +30 hit). |
| `remove_guard` | Strips the target's Guard before contact resolves (like the bypasses), so a strip-guard + attack lands its own hit. No damage of its own. |
| `bypass_dodge` | This attack ignores the target's Dodge (strips it before contact resolves). |
| `bypass_stealth` | This attack breaks the target's Stealth and reverses its +30 hit bonus. |
| `retaliate_if_hit` | Arms a counter: if the actor is hit (or contacted via guard/soak) next turn, they deal `retaliate_damage` back. |
| `retaliate_if_missed` | Same, but fires when the attacker misses, dodges, or whiffs against stealth. |
| `drain` | Cosmetic tag that swaps the heal tooltip "Heal" → "Drain". The actual life-steal gating is `heal_condition = on_hp_damage`, not this flag. |
| `requires_control_contact` | Pure-control delivery contract. Runs the capability-driven contact resolver before target-side control payloads and suppresses those payloads on a miss, dodge, stealth evade, spirit miss, or guard block. |
| `is_starter_move` | Adds the move to the PC's default seeded loadout. Also tells the AI selector this is a generic baseline (no class-repertoire weight bonus). See **Equipping a move**. |

The capability tags (`checks_spirit_gate`, `requires_hit_roll`,
`checks_guard`, `checks_stance`) are set the same way; see **Pipeline
shape via capability tags** above. There is no `bypass_guard` flag.

### Summon

```
POD_combat_move_set_summon = { RECIPE = <recipe> }
```

Sets `summons_combatant` plus `summon_recipe = flag:<recipe>`.
Mid-combat, the engine seats a new combatant on the actor's team, built
from the named splat recipe (`wraith` today, dispatched in
`pod_combat_apply_summon_recipe_effect`). Author with `TARGETING = self`
and no `hp_damage`; the summon resolves after the damage phase, so the
summoned unit acts the next round.

---

## The hidden decision

Every move has one hidden decision. It is not a thing the player clicks
in the decisions panel - `decision_group_type = hidden` keeps it out of
that list. Its only jobs are to **carry the card art** and to **describe
the move's unlock requirement** for the player. The picture becomes the
move's card art; the `is_valid` block is what the loadout editor
*displays* as "what you need to unlock this". It is **not** the gate
that decides equip-ability or combat use - that is the unlock
dispatcher (see **Unlock gating**).

### Structure

```
POD_combat_move_<skill_key> = {
    picture = { reference = @texture_atk }
    is_valid_showing_failures_only = {
        # unlock conditions go here (or always = yes)
    }
    decision_group_type = hidden ai_check_interval = 0 confirm_text = blank_line
}
```

| Line | Why it's there |
|---|---|
| `picture = { reference = @texture_* }` | Card art in the loadout editor and as the move-button background in combat. Pick a texture constant (see **Picking values** and **Constants reference**). |
| `is_valid_showing_failures_only = { ... }` | The **displayed** unlock requirement, not the actual gate (see **Unlock gating**). `showing_failures_only` makes the loadout editor spell out *which* trait or perk is missing. Keep it in sync with the real gate by hand. |
| `decision_group_type = hidden` | Keeps it out of the player decisions panel. |
| `ai_check_interval = 0` | The AI never evaluates it as a real decision; move selection runs through the combat AI, not the decision system. |
| `confirm_text = blank_line` | Suppresses a stray confirm prompt. |

The decision name **must** match the constructor's `DECISION` parameter
exactly, and its suffix must match `SKILL_KEY` (decision
`POD_combat_move_strike` -> `SKILL_KEY = strike`). See **The
constructor**.

No `desc` line is needed: CK3 auto-resolves `<decision_id>_desc`, and
the matching loc key routes that at the stat-card macro. See
**Localization**.

**The decision is not the gate.** What actually decides whether a
character can equip or use a move is the unlock dispatcher,
not this decision. That distinction is the biggest trap in
hand-authoring a move - see **Unlock gating** next.

### Common gate patterns

```
# No requirements - anyone can equip
is_valid_showing_failures_only = {
    always = yes
}

# Trait + perk (typical discipline move)
is_valid_showing_failures_only = {
    has_trait = celeritydiscipline
    has_perk = swift_strike_perk
}

# Splat aggregate (anyone with any fera trait)
is_valid_showing_failures_only = {
    POD_is_fera_trigger = yes
}

# Faith gate
is_valid_showing_failures_only = {
    faith = faith:blackfury
    has_perk = primal_howl_perk
}

# Advanced trait + specific perk
is_valid_showing_failures_only = {
    has_trait = serpentisadvanced
    has_perk = breath_of_the_basilisk_perk
}
```

### Texture constants

Pictures live as `@texture_*` constants at the top of
`pod_combat_move_decisions.txt`. Two families exist:

**Four category fallbacks** - use one of these for any generic move,
matched to its `CATEGORY`:

| Constant | Image file | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| `@texture_atk` | `decision_bloodsurge.dds` | ATK moves |
| `@texture_def` | `decision_warding_evil.dds` | DEF moves |
| `@texture_ctl` | `decision_eye_of_the_storm.dds` | CTL moves |
| `@texture_pwr` | `decision_blood_moon.dds` | PWR moves |

**Discipline / family rollups** - one per discipline or power family
(`@texture_celerity`, `@texture_serpentis`, `@texture_dominate`,
`@texture_obfuscate`, `@texture_bloodsorcery`, `@texture_demonology`,
`@texture_theurgy`, ...), each pointing at that discipline's
`decision_POD_<name>.dds` art, plus `@texture_faith` for covenant moves.
Prefer the family texture when the move belongs to a named discipline;
fall back to the category texture otherwise.

A hidden decision with no `picture` shows a generic placeholder, so
always set one.

