## Recipes

Each recipe lists what to set inside `scope:new_combat_move = { ... }`
plus the `TARGETING` and gate. See **Setter reference** for the exact
setter signatures.

**Basic single-enemy attack.** Set the four physical tags
(`checks_spirit_gate`, `requires_hit_roll`, `checks_guard`,
`checks_stance`), `hp_damage`, `TARGETING = single_enemy`. Damage class
optional.

**Discipline attack.** As above but add `energy_cost`, drop
`requires_hit_roll` and `checks_guard` (keep `checks_stance`) so it
auto-hits past guard while dodge/stealth still evade, and gate the
decision on the trait+perk. Often add an elemental damage class (fire,
silver, ...) so PoD lands it aggravated against the vulnerable splat.

**AoE attack.** Set `TARGETING = team_enemy`; the `hp_damage` value
applies to *each* enemy hit, so size it per-enemy. Cost should reflect
the spread - typically 5-6 AP and 2 energy. Add `bypass_dodge` so one
dodging enemy does not slip the whole sweep.

**Heal-on-hit / drain.** Pair `hp_damage` with `hp_heal`, then add
`heal_condition = on_hp_damage` so the heal only fires when the swing
lands (without it the heal fires even on a miss). Add the `drain` flag
if you also want the tooltip to read "Drain 2" instead of "Heal 2" - it
is cosmetic; the gating lives in `heal_condition`.

**Defensive stance.** `TARGETING = self`, set one stance flag
(`set_guard`, `dodge`, or `stealth`). No damage - stances spend AP to be
safer next turn. Cost 2-3 AP.

**Combat-long buff.** Use the `*_combat` tier setters instead of
`*_round`. Price them higher (they do not refresh) - 3-4 AP for a medium
tier, 4-5 for large.

**Debuff.** Use `*_debuff_round` / `*_debuff_combat` tier setters with
`TARGETING = single_enemy`. The AI already scores tier debuffs and
stacks them when favorable.

**Counter-attack stance.** Add the `retaliate_if_hit` (or
`retaliate_if_missed`) flag and set `retaliate_damage`; optionally pair
with `set_guard`. The actor strikes back at whoever triggers the flag
next turn. For an aggravated counter add `retaliate_agg = yes` (this
marks only the counter as aggravated; the primary damage's
classification is the move's own damage class).

**Finisher.** There is no built-in low-HP damage scaling, but high
`hp_damage` + high AP makes a natural closer. The AI's HP-finish bonus
(`@ai_hp_finish_1box`, +25 when the target is at its last box) already
biases it to pick this when it kills.

**AP cantrip.** `TARGETING = self`, set `ap_gain`. Keep `ap_gain` below
the `AP_COST` or you create a free-AP loop - `ap_gain N` at `ap_cost N`
is a wash, so make it `ap_cost N` for `ap_gain N-1` at most. Or attach
`ap_gain` to a damage move so it reads as an efficient attack rather
than an exploit.

